<section xml:id="sec-fem-mc-prelim" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Preliminaries</title>


  <!-- use hyperlinks to reference -->
   <!--first populate, then find-replace for the links.
   references to be reformatted added later.
   add references to end of each section as you go along.-->  

   <subsection xml:id="subsec-fem">
    <title>Finite Element Methods</title>
    <p>
        The finite element method (FEM) seeks an approximate weak solution 
        <m>u_h</m> to a PDE by selecting from finite-dimensional 
        subspace <m>V_h \subset V</m> of a suitable Hilbert space <m>V</m>\cite{pryer2022fem}.  
        For the model Poisson 
        problem <m>-\Delta u = f</m> on <m>\Omega</m> with <m>u|_{\partial\Omega} = 0</m>, 
        this gives: find <m>u \in H^1_0(\Omega)</m> such that
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-poisson-weak">
            a(u,v) := \int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla v\,\mathrm{d}x 
            = \int_\Omega f v\,\mathrm{d}x =: l(v) 
            \qquad \forall\, v \in H^1_0(\Omega).
        </md>
        The Lax-Milgram theorem guarantees a unique solution whenever 
        <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m> is coercive and continuous on <m>V</m>, and <m>l</m> 
        is a bounded linear functional. 
        Restricting to a conforming finite element space 
        <m>V_h \subset H^1_0(\Omega)</m> and choosing a basis 
        <m>\{\phi_i\}_{i=1}^{N_h}</m> yields the linear system 
        <m>\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{f}</m> with <m>A_{ij} = a(\phi_j,\phi_i)</m>. The coercivity and continuity of <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m> results in an  
        <m>\mathbf{A}</m> positive-definite and bounded. The C\'{e}a lemma provides the best-approximation 
        estimate
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-cea">
            \| u - u_h \|_V \;\leq\; \frac{\gamma}{\alpha} 
            \inf_{w_h \in V_h} \| u - w_h \|_V,
        </md>
        where <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\gamma</m> are the coercivity and continuity 
        constants of <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m> respectively.
    </p>
   </subsection>


   <subsection xml:id="subsec-supg">
    <title>SUPG Stabilisation</title>
    <p>
        For advection-dominated problems, the standard Galerkin 
        formulation becomes unstable.
        The bilinear form associated 
        with the operator <m>\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla u + \sigma u</m> 
        is not necessarily coercive in <m>H^1(\Omega)</m>, and the Galerkin solution may exhibit
        spurious oscillations~\cite{ern2004theory}. 
        The Streamline Upwind Petrov-Galerkin (SUPG) method~\cite{brooks1982streamline, johnson1983finite} 
        addresses this by modifying the test function to 
        <m>v + \delta_K \mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v</m> on each element <m>K</m>, 
        where <m>\delta_K > 0</m> is a mesh-dependent stabilisation 
        parameter.
    </p>
    
    <p>
        The effect of SUPG is to add an artificial diffusion of strength <m>\delta_K</m> along streamlines of the solution. Consistency is preserved - as the additional term vanishes when evaluating the exact solution - while coercivity is ensured for a sufficient strength <m>\delta_K</m>.
    </p>
   </subsection>

   <subsection xml:id="subsec-mc">
    <title>Monte Carlo Integration</title>
    <p>
        Monte Carlo integration estimates an integral 
        <m>I = \int_\Omega f(y)\,\mathrm{d}y</m> by the sample mean
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-mc-estimator">
            I_M := \frac{|\Omega|}{M}\sum_{j=1}^M f(Y_j), 
            \qquad Y_j \stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim} \mathrm{Unif}(\Omega).
        </md>
        The estimator is unbiased with <m>\mathbb{E}[I_M] = I</m>, and its 
        standard deviation satisfies
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-mc-error">
            \mathbb{E}\bigl[|I - I_M|^2\bigr]^{1/2} 
            = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(f)^{1/2}}{\sqrt{M}},
        </md>
        where <m>\mathrm{Var}(f) = \int_\Omega (f - I/|\Omega|)^2\,
        \mathrm{d}y</m>~\cite{gobet2016monte}. Notably, this <m>\mathcal{O}(M^{-1/2})</m> rate 
        is independent of dimension, making Monte Carlo the method 
        of choice for high-dimensional integration.
    </p>
   </subsection>

   <subsection xml:id="subsec-identities">
    <title>Useful Identities</title>
    <p>
        Finally, the following identities are used repeatedly in the analysis and stated here for convenience. 
        The first is the Green's formula for the advection operator. 
        Given <m>v, w \in H^1(\Omega)</m> and 
        <m>\mathbf{b} \in [W^{1,\infty}(\Omega)]^2</m>, we have
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-greens">
            \int_{\Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, w \, \mathrm{d}x
            = \int_{\partial \Omega} v w (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n})\,\mathrm{d}S
            - \int_{\Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla w)\, v\,\mathrm{d}x
            - \int_{\Omega} v w \, \nabla\cdot\mathbf{b}\,\mathrm{d}x .
        </md>
        For much of our analysis, <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is taken to be a constant advection direction, thus the final term vanishes.
    </p>

    <p>
        In addition, Young's inequality states that, for any <m>a,b\in\mathbb{R}</m> we have
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-youngs">
            |ab| \leq \frac{a^2}{2} + \frac{b^2}{2}.
        </md>
    </p>
   </subsection>

</section>